# scoursh scanner configuration - rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.6.1
#
# Exactly one record, whose id is the frozen literal `scanner`.  Every other key
# is optional and takes the stated default, so an absent file is equivalent to
# one containing only `id: scanner`.
#
# Values are DATA and are never expanded or executed: `$HOME` here is five
# literal bytes (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 26).
#
# THE NETWORK KEYS BELOW ARE CLAMPED FOR A RUNNING-ENDPOINT (DAST) SCAN.
# `requests-per-second`, `jobs`, `request-budget`, `circuit-breaker-failures`
# and `circuit-breaker-window` are resolved as usual and then held to a
# conservative module limit inside lib/http.sh, at the same chokepoint the
# scope gate lives at, because the host this tool is pointed at is one it
# cannot vet (docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md, "Safety defaults and authorisation").
# The effective DAST values are 4 requests/second, at most 4 requests in flight
# at once, and a per-run budget of 5000, so the `request-budget: 20000` below
# is the schema default for the run as a whole and is NOT what a DAST scan will
# actually spend: a DAST scan is clamped to 5000 whatever this line says.  That
# clamp is SILENT, because the value it clamps is the one an operator who never
# edited this file already has; a network run states the numbers it will
# actually keep on one informational line, and warns only about a value someone
# deliberately raised above a limit.  The rate, concurrency and breaker numbers
# below already equal their limits, so they are never clamped as shipped.
#
# `jobs` is the CONCURRENCY key, and it means two different things in two
# different places, which is worth knowing before raising it.  For a
# file-scanning module it is worker parallelism and nothing bounds it here; for
# a network module it is additionally the number of connections a target may
# see AT ONCE, so lib/http.sh holds it to the same conservative limit as the
# rest of this list.  Rate and concurrency are not the same control: a token
# bucket spaces requests out over time, and against a target where each request
# takes seconds a high `jobs` value still opens that many simultaneous
# connections while the average rate stays inside its ceiling.  A `scan.sh
# sast` run is unaffected by this clamp - it never reaches the chokepoint.
#
# `circuit-breaker-window` is bounded at BOTH ends: shorter than 60 seconds is
# raised to 60 (a shorter window counts fewer failures towards the same
# threshold, which is the weaker breaker), and anything longer than 86400 is
# lowered to a day, which for any real run is the same thing as a window that
# never expires.
#
# The limits are not removable here: there is no config key, environment
# variable, or flag that switches the budget or the breaker off, and no
# schema-valid value of any of these four keys can disable either of them.
#
# RAISING one of them is a different question from removing it, and the answer
# depends on WHERE the raised value came from.  A value set in THIS FILE, or
# left at its default, is clamped down to the conservative limit with one
# warning; a value given explicitly on the command line or in a
# SCOURSH_CONFIG_* environment variable is refused outright (exit 2), because
# scoursh does not run at a number other than the one you asked for.  That
# applies to `--jobs` too on a network run: `--jobs 16` against an unaffirmed
# DAST target is exit 2, not a silently-lowered 4.  To
# actually raise one, affirm that you own the target:
#
#   scan.sh dast --target NAME --i-own-target NAME
#
# That affirmation is per run and is never persisted: there is deliberately no
# key here that means "always unrestricted", and there never will be.  It also
# raises nothing by itself - it is a key that makes the higher settings
# available, not a switch that selects one.
#
# `contact` is what a target owner who notices your traffic uses to reach you.
# Every request carries `scoursh/<version> (+<contact>)`; with no contact set,
# the request still identifies the tool but cannot identify you.  One printable
# token, no spaces - an email address or a URL.  `--contact` is the flag form.
#
#   contact: security@example.org
#
# `recommended-header` is the DAST security-header roll-up list
# (modules/dast/passive/headers.sh's DAST-HDR-RECOMMENDED_MISSING-01):
# repeatable, one RFC 7230 header field-name per entry.  Set here, it replaces
# the shipped default entirely rather than adding to it - list every header you
# want reported, including any of the seven the shipped
# modules/dast/passive/recommended-headers.txt carries that you still want. A
# name a dedicated check already owns (content-security-policy,
# strict-transport-security, x-frame-options, x-content-type-options) is
# dropped from whichever list supplies it, since that absence already has its
# own finding. Left unset here, the roll-up falls back to
# SCOURSH_DAST_RECOMMENDED_HEADERS_FILE, then to the shipped list.
#
#   recommended-header: permissions-policy
#   recommended-header: cross-origin-opener-policy

id: scanner
requests-per-second: 4
jobs: 4
http-timeout: 20
max-redirects: 5
request-budget: 20000
circuit-breaker-failures: 10
circuit-breaker-window: 60
fail-on: none
min-confidence: low
redact-secrets: true
formats: json
formats: sarif
formats: html
formats: md
max-matches-per-file: 200
evidence-max-bytes: 512
state-retain-runs: 30
history-window-days: 365
history-max-commits: 5000
lock-stale-seconds: 30
mutex-timeout-seconds: 120
tls-expiry-warn-days: 30
notes: scratch-dir is best pointed at a tmpfs. Overwrite-based erasure is not
  effective on APFS, journalled ext4, or any SSD with wear levelling, so a
  memory-backed scratch directory is the real control for the raw matched lines
  scan_match writes there (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 9, finding F16).
